WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.300
The Untapped Therapeutic Frontier There are nine point five million possible drug-disease combinations.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.360
You've tested thirty-two thousand five hundred. That's like searching for your keys in zero point three four percent of

3
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:20.140
your house and declaring them lost. The other ninety-nine point seven percent is sitting right there, untested, because

4
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:27.440
the molecules weren't profitable enough to bother. How You Accidentally Cured Three Things and Then Stopped.

5
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:36.580
In nineteen ninety-six, a team of Pfizer scientists sat in a room looking at clinical trial data for a chest pain drug called

6
00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.880
sildenafil. The drug had failed. It did not fix chest pain.

7
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:49.380
Nobody's chest felt better. The trial was, by every metric that mattered to the people who paid for it, a complete waste

8
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.960
of money. Then the test subjects refused to return their leftover pills.

9
00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.000
This had never happened before. People don't hoard failed medicine.

10
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:09.140
The scientists investigated. What they discovered was that sildenafil had a side effect so commercially valuable it would

11
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.160
generate one point nine billion dollars per year for the next two decades.

12
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:23.680
You know it as Viagra. The cure for erectile dysfunction was hiding inside a failed heart drug, and the only reason anyone

13
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.900
found it was because a group of men did something unusual with a feedback form.

14
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.410
Minoxidil has a similar origin story. Developed for high blood pressure.

15
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.870
Failed at that too. Side effect: hair growth. It became Rogaine.

16
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.020
Then sildenafil got repurposed *again* for pulmonary hypertension, making it three cures from two failed drugs, none of

17
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:56.620
them intentional. On Wishonia, when we heard about this, we asked the obvious question: "So then you tested all the other

18
00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.180
drugs for all the other side effects, right?". You did not.

19
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:09.539
You found three cures by accident, said "huh, neat," and went back to testing the same twelve biological targets that pharmaceutical

20
00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:17.200
companies can patent. This is like finding buried treasure in your backyard, selling the treasure, and then never digging

21
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.430
in your backyard again because the first hole was supposed to be for a fence post.

22
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.850
I have been observing your species for eighty years. I have seen you do many confusing things.

23
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.340
But this one keeps me up at night, which is saying something because I don't sleep.

24
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.860
Your Medicine Cabinet (After five thousand years of Civilization).

25
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.380
Prescriptions: twenty thousand products approved in the U.S.

26
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.060
Sounds impressive until you realize most are variations of each other.

27
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:54.680
Your pharmaceutical industry has invented three hundred ways to sell the same painkiller in different-colored packaging.

28
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.620
On Wishonia, this would be considered a graphic design achievement, not a medical one.

29
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.440
Unique Ingredients: These products contain only one thousand six hundred and fifty compounds.

30
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.580
Fewer unique molecules than a French bakery uses ingredients.

31
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.960
Investigational Drugs: seven thousand five hundred compounds have passed Phase One globally.

32
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:23.240
These are compounds you already know won't kill people, sitting in filing cabinets, doing nothing.

33
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:29.580
Safe Stuff: The F.D.A. generally recognized as safe, or G.R.A.S.

34
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.140
list contains six hundred and thirty-five substances. Things you could eat right now without dying.

35
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:43.620
Low bar. Still useful. Total unique compounds humanity knows are safe-ish: nine thousand five hundred compounds.

36
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.600
That's it. Five millennia of civilization, and this is your entire toolkit.

37
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.500
On Wishonia, we have more compounds in a single bathroom cabinet.

38
00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.900
Your bathroom cabinet has shampoo and expired cough syrup from twenty nineteen.

39
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.860
Ways Your Body Breaks (The To-Do List Nobody's Doing). Codes: The I.C.D.-ten lists fourteen thousand ways your meat can

40
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.120
malfunction. This is your species' most comprehensive to-do list.

41
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:21.240
You are not doing it. Real Targets: After consolidation, one thousand trial-relevant diseases are actually worth testing

42
00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:27.190
drugs against. The Multiplication Problem. Here is where it gets uncomfortable.

43
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.280
Take your nine thousand five hundred safe compounds. Multiply by one thousand diseases.

44
00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:41.080
This gives you the total number of things you *could* test tomorrow morning if you weren't busy filling out forms about

45
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:49.040
whether you're allowed to test them. Nine thousand five hundred compounds times one thousand diseases equals nine point

46
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.180
five million possible drug-disease combinations. This is not a hard calculation.

47
00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:03.080
A child could do it. Several children have. The children were then told to stop asking uncomfortable questions and go play

48
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:13.920
outside. What You've Actually Tried. Approved uses: one thousand seven hundred and fifty unique approved drug-disease pairings.

49
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.570
This is your entire medical canon. Five thousand years of civilization.

50
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.700
Libraries of medical journals. Nobel Prizes. White coats. Stethoscopes.

51
00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:33.720
All of it produced this number. Repurposed uses: thirty percent of drugs gain at least one new indication after initial

52
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:42.300
approval. The rest never get a second date. Failed trials: Let's be generous and say you tested ten times as many things

53
00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:49.120
that failed. You learned nothing from most of them because your system throws away negative results like a restaurant that

54
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:56.510
only writes down the recipes that taste good. Total tested: thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships.

55
00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:59.800
The Number That Should Keep You Awake. Thirty-two thousand five hundred relationships tested out of nine point five million

56
00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:10.400
possibilities equals zero point three four two percent. where: **You have explored one-third of one percent of the things

57
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.780
you could test using molecules you already have.** I want to sit with that for a moment.

58
00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:23.760
You have safe compounds. You have sick people. You have the ability to introduce them to each other.

59
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.760
You have done this zero point three four two percent of the time.

60
00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.460
The other ninety-nine point six six percent of introductions have never been made.

61
00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.440
Not because they wouldn't work. Because the molecule didn't have a marketing budget.

62
00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:47.560
On Wishonia, if a doctor explored zero point three four two percent of treatment options and declared the patient incurable,

63
00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:55.700
we would gently suggest the doctor continue looking. On Earth, you publish a paper about the zero point three four two percent

64
00:06:55.700 --> 00:07:02.080
and apply for a grant to re-study the same zero point three four two percent you studied last time.

65
00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.160
"Maybe the Other ninety-nine point six six percent Is Useless".

66
00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:13.320
This is the objection I hear most often from humans who would prefer not to feel bad about this.

67
00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:19.660
Let me address it by introducing you to your own biology, which you have mostly not met.

68
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:26.260
You're ignoring eighty-eight percent of your own biology. Mapping three hundred and fifty thousand plus clinical trials

69
00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:31.080
showed that only twelve percent of the human interactome has ever been targeted by drugs.

70
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:35.120
You have a one hundred-room mansion. You live in the bathroom.

71
00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:40.230
You have decorated the bathroom very nicely. You have published papers about the bathroom.

72
00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:47.440
The other eighty-eight rooms are locked, and when someone asks about them, you say "we're focused on bathroom excellence

73
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.920
at this time.". Your drugs are promiscuous and you're pretending they're not.

74
00:07:52.800 --> 00:08:00.120
F.D.A.-approved drugs bind to things you didn't intend. This means "side effects" are frequently "cures for something else"

75
00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:04.480
that nobody bothered to notice. Remember Viagra? That was a side effect.

76
00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:16.220
Rogaine? Side effect. Sildenafil got repurposed *again* for pulmonary hypertension: three cures from two drugs, none intentional.

77
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.140
Your entire drug discovery strategy is "wander around and hope you trip over something."

78
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:28.550
It works surprisingly often, which is less a compliment to the strategy than an indictment of having no strategy at all.

79
00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:35.179
Your diseases share wiring you haven't traced. Diseases cluster on shared biological networks.

80
00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:44.039
Arthritis and Alzheimer's share pathways. Lithium was prescribed for gout in the eighteen hundreds; decades later, it turned

81
00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:47.230
out to be the gold standard treatment for bipolar disorder.

82
00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:51.840
Nobody connected the dots because nobody was drawing lines between diseases.

83
00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.020
The drug that treats your grandfather's inflammation might fix your grandmother's neurodegeneration.

84
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.660
You won't find out, because testing it would require a one hundred million dollar clinical trial that takes longer than

85
00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:09.340
Grandma has left. Beyond Your Cupboard (Where It Gets Embarrassing).

86
00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:13.260
Everything above only counted molecules you *already have*.

87
00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.900
The safe stuff. The things sitting in your filing cabinets right now, gathering dust and expiration dates.

88
00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:27.680
If you look at what's chemically possible, it gets worse. Chemical Space: Estimated ten to the twenty-third power to ten

89
00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.640
to the sixtieth power drug-like molecules. More candidates than atoms in your body.

90
00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:40.640
More than stars in your galaxy. More than the number of times your regulatory agencies have said "we need more data.".

91
00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:47.300
Virtual Libraries: ZINC twenty-two contains tens of billions of purchasable virtual molecules.

92
00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:53.000
You can order them online. Like shoes, but for not dying. You have ordered more shoes.

93
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.980
Synthesized: You have made less than ten to the negative fortieth power of possible molecules.

94
00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:08.040
Tested divided by Possible equals approximately ten to the negative twentieth power to ten to the negative fiftieth power.

95
00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.940
These are numbers so small that the notation system itself starts to feel embarrassed.

96
00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:19.840
Ten to the negative fiftieth power is not a fraction. It's a rounding error's rounding error.

97
00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:25.820
It is the mathematical equivalent of having not started. Because you have not started.

98
00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:35.860
Why You Are Still Sick (A Diagnosis). I want to be very clear: you are not sick because curing disease is impossible.

99
00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:44.660
You are sick because you built a system that charges one hundred million dollars per introduction between a molecule and

100
00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:51.780
a disease, and then gave all the money to a different system that introduces warheads to cities.

101
00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:58.640
The warheads work fine. The disease introduction system has four design flaws.

102
00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.880
All of them are about money. None of them are about science.

103
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:13.340
The ticket price is insane. A typical Phase Two to Three trial costs thirty to one hundred million dollars (median cost

104
00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:21.720
per patient forty-one thousand dollars). At that price, you only test things pharmaceutical companies can patent and sell

105
00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:32.300
at enormous markups. Everything else sits untested. Imagine a dating app where each date costs one hundred million dollars

106
00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:38.300
and only billionaires are allowed to swipe. That's your drug approval system.

107
00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:44.200
The molecules without money die alone. Everyone goes to France.

108
00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:51.760
Trials overwhelmingly test the same few biological targets due to preferential attachment dynamics.

109
00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:57.500
Everyone studies what everyone else studies because that's how you get grants.

110
00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:05.600
It's a herd behavior that would embarrass actual herds. Cows at least spread out across the field.

111
00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:13.340
Your researchers cluster around twelve targets because those are the targets with grant funding, and the grant funding goes

112
00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:17.250
to those targets because those are the targets with researchers.

113
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.440
This is a circle. Not the good kind. Cheap molecules can't play.

114
00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:30.920
Most molecules have no patent. No patent means no one hundred million dollar trial.

115
00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:39.740
No trial means no approval. Metformin, a diabetes drug from the nineteen-fifties, shows persistent signals for reducing

116
00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:45.820
cancer risk and extending lifespan. It costs pennies. It's off-patent.

117
00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:55.120
The trial to prove it works for aging (TAME) spent years begging for funding because no pharmaceutical company will bankroll

118
00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:02.980
a drug they can't monopolize. The molecule that could save your life doesn't make enough money to be worth testing.

119
00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:11.310
You die. Quarterly earnings go up. The system is working exactly as designed, which is the problem.

120
00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:19.660
Unprofitable diseases don't exist. If you have a rare disease with no market, you don't get a drug.

121
00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:29.480
Your cells malfunction in an unprofitable way. The invisible hand of the market has weighed your life against shareholder

122
00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:35.930
returns and found you wanting. On Wishonia, we don't have an invisible hand.

123
00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:43.730
We have a visible calculator. It counts deaths. When the number goes up, it allocates resources.

124
00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:51.540
This is apparently "radical" on your planet. The Timeline (How Long Until You Finish Looking).

125
00:13:52.480 --> 00:14:00.280
At your current pace of three point three thousand trials per year, I calculated how long it would take to systematically

126
00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:08.300
test what you have. The results are, and I say this with genuine affection for your species, not great.

127
00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:18.880
Just the safe compounds you already own: Nine point five million combinations divided by three point three thousand trials

128
00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:27.080
per year equals two point eight eight thousand years. Two thousand eight hundred and eighty years.

129
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:32.680
Using compounds *already confirmed not to kill people*. Sitting in cabinets.

130
00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:39.940
With labels on them. You won't finish until roughly the year five thousand, at which point the people who needed the drugs

131
00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.820
will have been dead for three thousand years, which somewhat reduces the clinical urgency.

132
00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:54.680
Combination therapies (which is how modern medicine actually works): Oncology, H.I.V., cardiology, psychiatry: all routinely

133
00:14:54.680 --> 00:15:00.180
use multi-drug cocktails. The real question isn't "does this one drug work?"

134
00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:09.260
but "which combinations work?" Testing pairwise combinations: Forty-five point one billion combinations divided by three

135
00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:15.420
thousand three hundred trials per year equals thirteen point seven million years.

136
00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:19.640
That's longer than *Homo sapiens* has existed as a species.

137
00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:26.680
You would need to have started testing drug combinations before your ancestors climbed down from the trees, and you still

138
00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:32.120
wouldn't be done. And that's only pairs. If you want triplets, you'll need to outlive the sun.

139
00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:41.460
The full chemical universe: Beyond your cupboard, the chemical space contains ten to the twenty-third to ten to the sixtieth

140
00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:49.880
power drug-like molecules. At current trial capacity, exploring even the synthesizable fraction would take longer than the

141
00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:55.760
universe has existed. The universe is thirteen point eight billion years old.

142
00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:02.430
You need more time than that. To test pills. But Won't We Run Out of Easy Discoveries?

143
00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:09.740
Some of your economists (the ones who've never looked through a microscope) will argue "diminishing returns."

144
00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:16.320
The more you search, the harder each new find becomes. This assumes you've already picked the low-hanging fruit.

145
00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:24.880
You are not in the orchard. You are standing outside the orchard filling out a forty-seven-page application to enter the

146
00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:32.060
orchard. The application requires three letters of recommendation, an institutional review board approval, and a fourteen-month

147
00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:36.700
waiting period. The fruit is rotting. You can see it rotting.

148
00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:45.120
You're on page twelve of the application. Diminishing returns kicks in after you've explored a meaningful fraction of the

149
00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:52.760
space. At less than one percent explored, every trial teaches you something new, improving your ability to predict what

150
00:16:52.760 --> 00:17:01.760
works next. The math: even with weak learning effects, diminishing returns only dominates after exploring roughly thirty-seven

151
00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:09.540
percent of therapeutic space. For combination therapies, that crossover is over five million years away at current pace

152
00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:16.099
(thirty-seven percent of forty-five point one billion combinations at three thousand three hundred trials per year).

153
00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:20.940
You cannot have diminishing returns when you haven't had any returns.

154
00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:27.300
See the formal analysis for the equations. What Happens When You Actually Try.

155
00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:34.540
The Oxford RECOVERY Trial during COVID-nineteen proved something your pharmaceutical industry would prefer you didn't know:

156
00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:43.020
large-scale pragmatic trials can run for roughly five hundred dollars per patient and deliver results in less than one hundred

157
00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:48.040
days. Five hundred dollars versus forty-one thousand dollars.

158
00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:52.480
Read those two numbers again. One of them is the cost of finding out if a drug works.

159
00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:59.020
The other is the cost of finding out if a drug works while also paying for marble lobbies, compliance departments, and a

160
00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:04.200
CEO's third yacht. The medicine is the same. The patients are the same.

161
00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:13.520
The yacht is new. With a decentralized F.D.A., trial capacity increases by twelve point three times: Nine point five million

162
00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:20.700
combinations divided by forty thousand seven hundred trials per year equals two hundred and thirty-four years.

163
00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:27.460
Still not fast. But at least it's a number your grandchildren might live to see the end of, rather than a number that requires

164
00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:35.640
your species to outlive its own star. The first few decades would capture the highest-value discoveries, the cures that

165
00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:40.300
current trial scarcity forces you to ignore while people die in the waiting room.

166
00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:47.060
Your decentralized F.D.A. builds permanent infrastructure for systematic therapeutic exploration.

167
00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:54.020
The goal isn't to "finish." The goal is to stop leaving cures on the table while you form subcommittees to discuss whether

168
00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:57.960
to form committees to review the proposal to consider looking at them.

169
00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:08.400
The Bottom Line. You have tested zero point three four two percent of the drug-disease combinations testable with compounds

170
00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:16.640
you already own. If you include the larger chemical universe: roughly one-hundred-quintillionth to one-hundred-tredecillionth

171
00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:21.260
of what's possible. The reason is not scientific. The reason is financial.

172
00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:29.360
Trials cost too much and the money is busy being missiles. A decentralized, automated pragmatic trial system is the only

173
00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:35.660
route to systematically explore the remaining ninety-nine point nine nine nine percent of medical space.

174
00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.820
The cures exist. The molecules are sitting in your cabinets.

175
00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:46.680
The patients are willing. The only thing between them is a system that charges one hundred million dollars to introduce

176
00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:54.720
them and takes seventeen years to make the introduction. On Wishonia, we have a word for holding sick people hostage until

177
00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:59.410
someone pays a ransom. We call it crime. On Earth, you call it "regulation."

178
00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:01.840
You seem very proud of it.
